### How do of() and ofNullable() differ?

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.Optional;

public class OfVsOfNullable {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // of: value MUST be non-null
        String accountId = "SAV2001";
        Optional<String> opt1 = Optional.of(accountId); // OK

        // of(null) throws NPE immediately
        try {
            Optional<String> opt2 = Optional.of(null); // NPE!
        } catch (NullPointerException e) {
            System.out.println("of(null): " + e.getClass().getSimpleName());
        }

        // ofNullable: null becomes empty
        Optional<String> opt3 = Optional.ofNullable(null);
        System.out.println("ofNullable(null): " + opt3); // Optional.empty

        Optional<String> opt4 = Optional.ofNullable("SAV2001");
        System.out.println("ofNullable(SAV2001): " + opt4); // Optional[SAV2001]
    }
}
```

```text
of(null): NullPointerException
ofNullable(null): Optional.empty
ofNullable(SAV2001): Optional[SAV2001]
```

### When to use which?

| Scenario | Use | Why |
|----------|-----|-----|
| Value from your own logic (should never be null) | of() | Null = bug, fail fast |
| Value from database/API (might be null) | ofNullable() | Null = not found |
| Value from user input (might be missing) | ofNullable() | Null = not provided |
| JPA save result (should never be null) | of() | Null = JPA bug |
| Map.get() result (might be absent) | ofNullable() | Null = key not found |

### How do you use ofNullable at system boundaries?

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.Map;
import java.util.Optional;

public class BoundaryExample {
    // Legacy API returns null for missing accounts
    static String legacyFindName(String id) {
        Map<String, String> db = Map.of("SAV2001", "Rajesh");
        return db.get(id); // returns null if not found
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Convert null to Optional at the boundary
        String name = Optional.ofNullable(legacyFindName("SAV9999"))
            .map(String::toUpperCase)
            .orElse("UNKNOWN");

        System.out.println("Name: " + name); // UNKNOWN
    }
}
```

### What happens with Optional.empty()?

```java
// Optional.empty() creates an empty Optional directly
Optional<String> empty = Optional.empty();
System.out.println(empty.isPresent()); // false
System.out.println(empty.orElse("default")); // default

// Use empty() when you explicitly want to signal absence
public Optional<Account> findAccount(String id) {
    if (id == null || id.isBlank()) {
        return Optional.empty(); // explicit absence
    }
    return Optional.ofNullable(repository.findById(id));
}
```

### What are common mistakes?

```java
// WRONG: using of() for data from external sources
Optional<String> opt = Optional.of(request.getParameter("name")); // NPE if null!

// CORRECT: use ofNullable for external data
Optional<String> opt = Optional.ofNullable(request.getParameter("name"));

// WRONG: wrapping a nullable in of() to "catch bugs"
String value = map.get("key"); // might be null
Optional<String> opt = Optional.of(value); // NPE if null!

// CORRECT: ofNullable for map lookups
Optional<String> opt = Optional.ofNullable(map.get("key"));
```

### How do of() and ofNullable() work internally?

```java
// Simplified internal implementation:
public static <T> Optional<T> of(T value) {
    return new Optional<>(Objects.requireNonNull(value)); // NPE on null
}

public static <T> Optional<T> ofNullable(T value) {
    return value == null ? empty() : of(value); // null -> empty, non-null -> wrapped
}
```

### What about Optional.or() (Java 9)?

```java
// or() provides a fallback Optional (lazy)
Optional<String> result = findInCache("SAV2001")
    .or(() -> findInDatabase("SAV2001"))
    .or(() -> findInLegacy("SAV2001"));
// Tries cache first, then DB, then legacy. Stops at first present.
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- of() is a bug detector. Use it when null indicates a programming error in your own code.
- ofNullable() is a safe wrapper. Use it at system boundaries where your code meets external data.
- The decision rule: ask 'is null a bug or a valid absence?' Bug -> of(). Valid absence -> ofNullable().
- In Spring applications, @Nullable annotations document which parameters may be null, guiding the of/ofNullable choice.
